WEBVTT

00:00:32.910 --> 00:01:00.000
One moment, all is quiet in this reserve fleet berthing area, but the next, orders flash

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:09.290
to a nearby shipyard, attacked by underwater atomic bomb. Prepare to handle

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:20.050
ships for decontamination. The handling of contaminated ships requires special

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:24.180
facilities and procedures. The vessels may be brought into a part of the

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:30.000
shipyard known as the decontamination zone, an area fenced off where workers

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.340
Workers and activities are isolated and carefully controlled

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:37.820
to avoid the spread of contamination.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.400
All personnel must pass through a change station

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.890
before entering and leaving the decontamination zone.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.410
This control point may be a floating barge

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:56.680
converted to a change house or a special building ashore.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.000
On entering the change station, every man is required to

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.250
stripped to the skin and changed to special clothing and equipment for

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:08.910
protection against getting contaminated material on his body or from getting it

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:18.520
into his system through breathing, eating, or getting it into cuts. By wearing film

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:23.150
badges and dosimeters, the dose a workman receives is measured and recorded.

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:30.000
These records help guard against overexposure. This is the initial survey and

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.840
boarding party which takes along survey instruments to detect and measure

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.550
radiation and determine the condition of the ship. It consists of a ship

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:43.470
superintendent, qualified to handle radiological decontamination, the pilot,

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:50.560
monitoring teams, deckhands, and advanced planning people. Parts of the tug which

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.780
may become contaminated by contact with the contaminated vessel should be

00:02:54.780 --> 00:03:00.000
adaptable to easy cleaning. Hull surfaces should be well painted and free from

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:09.060
scale, rust, and marine growths. Hemp or jute fenders should be covered. Rubber tire

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:18.120
fenders are easy to decontaminate. Such precautions are very important, not only for the safety of

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:30.000
the people aboard, but also for the safety of others who work in the shipyard. As the

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.350
tugs approach the contaminated ship in its berthing area, the monitors use their

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:44.850
survey instruments to determine the radiation level. As a result of data given

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:48.150
him by the monitors, the ship superintendent determines that the

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.070
radiation level of the ship is not too high for the ship to be boarded

00:03:52.070 --> 00:04:00.000
immediately. As the tug moves up against the contaminated hull, the value of ease of

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.790
cleaned fenders becomes apparent. Ladders, lines, and everything else that

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:12.900
contacts contaminated surfaces must be either easy to clean or disposable. The

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:27.260
monitors go aboard first to determine the level of radioactivity topside. To

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.000
avoid exposing its personnel unnecessarily to

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:39.330
radiation, the tug stands off while the monitors make their survey. The monitoring

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:46.190
teams take readings at various points topside. Readings are chalked on the

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:54.270
surfaces as a guide to the boarding party that will follow them. The radiation

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:59.130
readings are also reported by walkie-talkie to the ship superintendent on

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.000
the lead tug.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.600
who evaluates and records the readings on the ship's plans

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.440
in order to give a clear picture of general radiation levels topside.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:14.200
Beside providing the ship's superintendent with radiation readings,

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.550
the monitoring teams collect samples of radioactive dirt

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.050
for further study and analysis of the existing dust hazard

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.510
as required by the medical department.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:29.760
These samples must be carefully labeled for proper identification later.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.960
When the monitoring teams have finished their survey, the tug comes alongside to take them

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:50.910
aboard. As they are tracking contamination from the ship, they stop on a marked area,

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:55.720
take off their booties and gloves, and drop them in a disposable receptacle.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:00.000
Note the procedure. They take great care to

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.980
avoid any skin contact with contaminated clothing. Clothing is checked for

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:16.090
radioactive particles and so is equipment. The men also take showers. Every

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:20.750
precaution is observed to control radioactive particles and to keep them

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:28.370
from being spread to clean areas. These precautions only begin here. After

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.000
returning to the decontamination

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.700
zone of the shipyard, the men will be processed through the change station.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:40.260
The results of the monitor's survey are reported to the command center for a decision whether

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:45.200
to moor the ship in the yard or at a quarantine anchorage.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:52.950
Decontamination procedures and precautions at either site are the same.

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:59.760
If the ship is ordered into the yard, the decontamination may be done at a pier, slip, or dry dock.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:11.320
Deckhands go aboard to handle the lines and prepare the ship for towing.

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:21.790
Although a ship may become contaminated well out of range of heavy physical damage,

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:27.540
some light structures may be affected by the blast and rendered unsalvable.

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.800
As they are contaminated and will sink,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.660
It is best to dispose of them in the contaminated area before bringing the vessel into a clean shipyard.

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:55.850
The members of the party are guided in moving about the deck by the radiation markings left by the monitors to ensure that they receive a smaller dose of radiation as practicable.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.000
The advanced planning and survey party which includes shipyard personnel,

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.380
probably representatives of the reserve fleet and others,

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:15.640
look over the ship and estimate the work to be done.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:20.920
Any lines secured to the ship may become contaminated.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.250
As the manila or spring lay tow lines cannot be decontaminated,

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.000
they must not come in contact with the ship.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Wire slings at the ends

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:47.360
prevent this. Unlike the lines, the slings can be decontaminated. The deckhands and members of the

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:52.420
survey party return to the tug as their presence is not required on the contaminated ship.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:05.860
With the pilot directing the operation, the ship is towed to the shipyard decontamination

00:09:05.860 --> 00:09:12.480
zone.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.520
The dry dock, as well as the decontamination zone which surrounds it, is planned, outfitted,

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:24.750
and operated not only to facilitate decontamination, but to avoid the spread of radioactive particles.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:30.000
The manila lines used here also have wire slings at their ends to prevent direct contact

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.400
with the contaminated ship.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:40.160
Like the boarding and survey party,

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.860
the docking party should wear special clothing,

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:44.830
film badges and dosimeters.

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:18.070
Everything that contacts the contaminated ship should be distinctively marked

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:23.260
and is easy to decontaminate as practicable.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:26.310
Yard services must be supplied temporarily.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.390
However, even portable blowers must not be operated until the compartments have been monitored,

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:42.960
dust conditions evaluated, and the spaces declared safe for their use.

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:56.080
The underwater body in the dry dock should be monitored immediately to permit calculation of acceptable work periods.

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:59.540
Later, there will be detailed monitoring throughout the ship.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:07.070
Marine growths tend to hold contamination.

00:11:07.070 --> 00:11:11.730
They should not be allowed to dry out as this may make an inhalation hazard.

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:17.790
They should be removed while wet with long handle scrapers.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:21.450
These growths drop into the dry dock where they are kept wet.

00:11:21.450 --> 00:11:26.340
They are then disposed of with caution and the dry dock decontaminated as necessary.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.370
When the men are ready to secure, they must pass through the change station as they leave the decontamination zone.

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:45.320
This applies to the boarding and survey party, the docking gang, and everybody else in the zone.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:56.860
In the change station, the men take off their special clothing and leave it, as well as their decontamination equipment.

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.860
They wash themselves thoroughly with soap and water,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.360
remove any radioactive particles which may have gotten through the special clothing.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.100
Glasses are the only personal articles which men are allowed to take through the change

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:13.520
station.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.030
Then they must be checked and passed by the monitor to make sure they are free of radioactive

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:27.140
particles before they are allowed to pass into the clean area of the change station

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.700
to put on their regular clothes.

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:46.910
The change station procedures are a rigid safeguard to prevent decontamination workers from carrying away radioactive particles.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:56.490
In summary, the initial survey and boarding party must be equipped with special clothing and gear as well as survey instruments.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:04.480
All equipment which comes in contact with the ship should be distinctively marked

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:08.770
and is easy to decontaminate as practicable.

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:12.870
All shipyard facilities and equipment contacting the contaminated ship

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:16.580
should be distinctively marked and easy to decontaminate.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:20.600
Remember, we can't see radiation, feel it, or hear it.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.290
We can detect it and measure it.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:30.000
And what's more important, we can work with it without risk if we use our heads

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:32.660
and follow the prescribed procedures.
